{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1148 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ec0ac27053cb429c8acf65f5e99ff57f3467ddb356736b8653944210f9548dea" :edb/db-before-t 1147 :edb/db-after-t 1148 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534460 50 #inst "2026-10-11T21:23:04.292Z" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186154158 1078 #edb/ref 17592186154159 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186154162 1075 #edb/ref 17592186154164 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186154162 1075 #edb/ref 17592186154165 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186154162 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422509 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186154162 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422665 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186154162 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422509 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157013 1071 "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n$5n+4$ *is odd if* $n$ *is odd.*\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nLet $n$ be an $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}$ integer. Then, $n=\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{2a+1}}$ for some integer $a.$\n\nSubstituting $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{n=2a+1}}$ into the expression $5n+4$, we can show that $5n+4=\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{2b+1}},$ where $b$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{an integer}}}.$\n\nTherefore, since $5n+4$ $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{is one more than}}}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that $5n+4$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}.$" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157013 1071 "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n> $5n+4$ *is odd if* $n$ *is odd.*\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\n> Let $n$ be an $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}$ integer. Then, $n=\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{2a+1}}$ for some integer $a$.\n> Substituting $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{n=2a+1}}$ into the expression $5n+4$, we can show that $5n+4=\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{2b+1}}$, where $b$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{an integer}}}$.\n> Therefore, since $5n+4$ $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{is one more than}}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $5n+4$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}$." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186157013 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 5n+4 \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\n> *Let $n$ be an $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ integer. Then, $n=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a+1}$ for some integer $a$.*\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nn=2a+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $5n+4$ and show it is *odd* by writing it as *one more than* a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Substituting $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}n=2a+1}$ into the expression $5n+4$, we can show that $5n+4=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2b+1}$, where $b$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{an integer}}$.*\n\nOnce we've shown this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, since $5n+4$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{is one more than}}$ a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $5n+4$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$.*" 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186157013 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn \\text{ is odd}\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n5n+4 \\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\n*Let $n$ be an $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ integer. Then, $n=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a+1}$ for some integer $a.$*\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression $5n+4$ and show it is *odd* by writing it as *one more than* a multiple of $2.$\n\n*Substituting $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}n=2a+1}$ into the expression $5n+4$, we can show that $5n+4=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2b+1},$ where $b$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{an integer}}.$*\n\nOnce we've shown this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n*Therefore, since $5n+4$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{is one more than}}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that $5n+4$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}.$*" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157014 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n$n$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\n> Let $n$ be an even integer. Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression $15n+7$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $15n + 7|= 15(2a) + 7; = 30a + 7; = (30a + 6) + 1; = 2(15a + 3)_(⏟)_(b) + 1$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nb = 15a+3\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n15n+7 = 2b+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $15n+7$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $b = 15a+3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $15n + 7|= 2(15a + 3)_(⏟)_(b) + 1; = 2b + 1$\n> which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $15n+7$ is odd." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186157014 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n![](images/02/022c08614f15f942c5bb86928913458821e40c16549e7d08c70140db1eae776f.svg)\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nLet $n$ be an even integer. Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression $15n+7$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15n+7 & =15(2a)+7 \\\\  & =30a+7 \\\\  & =(30a+6)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(15a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b = 15a+3$ (highlighted above), we have that $15n+7 = 2b+1,$ which shows that $15n+7$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b = 15a+3.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15n+7 & =2{{(15a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $15n+7$ is odd." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157054 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n$n$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\n> If $n$ is odd, then $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nn=2a+1\n$$\n\ninto the expression $7n-1$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $7n - 1|= 7(2a + 1) - 1; = 14a + 7 - 1; = 14a + 6; = 2(7a + 3)_(⏟)_(b)$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nb=7a+3\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that $7n-1 = 2b$, which shows that $7n-1$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $b = 7a+3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $7n - 1|= 2(7a + 3)_(⏟)_(b); = 2b$\n> which is a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $7n-1$ is even." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186157054 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n![](images/74/74c3f2e4e2896418d438fee5f612965fe64e2bd7ecd5d7f92a24a3b7594e7092.svg)\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into the expression $7n-1$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n-1 & =7(2a+1)-1 \\\\  & =14a+7-1 \\\\  & =14a+6 \\\\  & =2{{(7a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=7a+3$ (highlighted above), we have that $7n-1 = 2b,$ which shows that $7n-1$ is indeed a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b = 7a+3.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n-1 & =2{{(7a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b} \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $7n-1$ is even." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157055 1071 "Prove that $n^2 -  2$ is odd if $n$ is odd." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157055 1071 "Prove that $n^2 - 2$ is odd if $n$ is odd." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186157055 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n$n$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\n> Since $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nn=2a+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $n^2 - 2$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Now, writing $n^2 - 2$ in terms of $a$, we obtain the following:\n> $n^{2} - 2|= (2a + 1)^{2} - 2; = (4a^{2} + 4a + 1) - 2; = (4a^{2} + 4a - 2) + 1; = 2(2a^{2} + 2a - 1)_(⏟)_(b) + 1$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nb=2a^2 + 2a - 1\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nn^2 - 2 = 2b+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $n^2 - 2$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $b=2a^2 + 2a - 1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $n^{2} - 2|= 2(2a^{2} + 2a - 1)_(⏟)_(b) + 1; = 2b + 1$\n> which is one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $n^2 - 2$ is odd." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186157055 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n![](images/b3/b346c9920bcd64b50c80e44521c87dc10a503b09c76867f45a78854aee22ce4e.svg)\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression $n^2 -  2$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nNow, writing $n^2 -  2$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & =(2a+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a+1)-2 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a-2)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=2a^2 + 2a  - 1$ (highlighted above), we have that $n^2 -  2 = 2b+1,$ which shows that $n^2 -  2$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2a^2 + 2a  - 1.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $n^2 -  2$ is odd." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186422736 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186422738 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186422737 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186422740 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186422741 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186422739 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157468 1050 780.0 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422692 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422703 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422711 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422715 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422729 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422731 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157642 1071 "Prove that if $n$ is odd, then $3n + 5$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n =$ {{field-1}} for {{field-2}}." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186157642 1071 "Prove that if $n$ is odd, then $3n+5$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=\\,${{field-1}} for {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+5 & ={{field-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-4}}. Since $a$ is {{field-5}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-6}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+5 & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $3n+5$ is {{field-8}}." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186157642 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,3n+5\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into the expression $3n+5$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+5 & =3(2a+1)+5 \\\\  & =6a+3+5 \\\\  & =6a+8 \\\\  & =2{{(3a+4)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=3a+4$ (highlighted above), we have that $3n+5=2b,$ which shows that $3n+5$ is indeed a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=3a+4.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+5 & =2{{(3a+4)}_{⏟}}_{b} \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $3n+5$ is even." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186158570 1064 "We now begin our journey into mathematical proof writing. Let's start by introducing some important terminology:\n\n*An **axiom** is a mathematical statement that is assumed to be true without requiring proof.*   \n\nAxioms are the central underpinnings of **axiomatic systems.** All provable results within a system can be derived from a set of axioms using logical reasoning. \n\nFor example, most of the geometry you've studied up to this point refers to geometry within flat, two-dimensional space, otherwise known as **Euclidean Geometry**.\n\nThere are five axioms from which the *entire system* of Euclidean Geometry is based:\n\n- **Axiom 1:** *Given two distinct points, there is a line segment that joins them.*\n\n- **Axiom 2:** *Any line segment can be extended into a straight line.*\n\n- **Axiom 3:** *Given two distinct points, we can construct a circle where one point lies on the circle and whose center is at the other point.*\n\n- **Axiom 4:** *All right angles are congruent.*\n\n- **Axiom 5:** *Given a line and a point that does not lie on the line, there exists exactly one line through the point that's parallel to the line.*\n\nEverything you know about Euclidean geometry can be proved starting from just these five axioms!\n\nA **mathematical proof** is a rigorous argument that a statement is true based on a set of axioms and previously established results. \n\nMathematical proofs follow a logical sequence of steps, starting from these foundational principles and building upon them using algebraic techniques and logic to arrive at a conclusion. Any mathematical proof aims to provide a watertight, convincing argument that a statement holds true." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186158570 1064 "We now begin our journey into mathematical proof writing. Let's start by introducing some important terminology:\n\n> *An **axiom** is a mathematical statement that is assumed to be true without requiring proof.*\n\nAxioms are the central underpinnings of **axiomatic systems.** All provable results within a system can be derived from a set of axioms using logical reasoning.\n\nFor example, most of the geometry you've studied up to this point refers to geometry within flat, two-dimensional space, otherwise known as **Euclidean Geometry**.\n\nThere are five axioms from which the *entire system* of Euclidean Geometry is based:\n\n- **Axiom 1:** *Given two distinct points, there is a line segment that joins them.*\n- **Axiom 2:** *Any line segment can be extended into a straight line.*\n- **Axiom 3:** *Given two distinct points, we can construct a circle where one point lies on the circle and whose center is at the other point.*\n- **Axiom 4:** *All right angles are congruent.*\n- **Axiom 5:** *Given a line and a point that does not lie on the line, there exists exactly one line through the point that's parallel to the line.*\n\nEverything you know about Euclidean geometry can be proved starting from just these five axioms!\n\nA **mathematical proof** is a rigorous argument that a statement is true based on a set of axioms and previously established results.\n\nMathematical proofs follow a logical sequence of steps, starting from these foundational principles and building upon them using algebraic techniques and logic to arrive at a conclusion. Any mathematical proof aims to provide a watertight, convincing argument that a statement holds true." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186158575 1064 "Consider the following statement:\n\n![](images/2c/2c1b99730dd8ff2296e3c8e36d3236ff45c41b8835a70d36b7f2302895d233c5.svg)\n\nNotice that this is an (implicit) universal statement of the form \n\n$$\nP \\Rightarrow Q \\quad \\equiv \\quad  \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, P(n) \\Rightarrow Q(n).\n$$\n\nLet's outline a proof schema (or template) for constructing a proof of this result:\n\n- **Step 1**: We assume $P$ is true. In other words, we let $n$ be an even integer.\n\n- **Step 2:** Next, we use the *definition* of evenness to write $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\n- **Step 3:** Then, we substitute $n=2a$ into the expression for $3n+2$ and use algebraic techniques to show that the expression can be written as $2b,$ where $b$ is an integer.\n\n- **Step 4:** Since $3n+2 = 2b$ is a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that it's even (i.e., that $Q$ is true).\n\nNote the following:\n\n- For now, we're simply constructing a \"bird's-eye view\" of how direct proof works. We haven't yet proved anything! We'll get to the details of the proof shortly.\n\n- The example concerns a statement of the form \n\n$$\n\\textrm{even}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{even}.\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use the same proof template to prove parity statements of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{even}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{odd}, \\qquad \\textrm{odd}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{even}, \\qquad \\textrm{odd}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{odd}\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that we'll also need to use the definition of an odd integer. Let's see an example of this." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186158575 1064 "Consider the following statement:\n\n$$\nn\n$$\n\nNotice that this is an (implicit) universal statement of the form\n$P \\Rightarrow Q \\quad \\equiv \\quad \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, P(n) \\Rightarrow Q(n)$.\n\nLet's outline a proof schema (or template) for constructing a proof of this result:\n\n- **Step 1**: We assume $P$ is true. In other words, we let $n$ be an even integer.\n- **Step 2:** Next, we use the *definition* of evenness to write $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.\n- **Step 3:** Then, we substitute $n=2a$ into the expression for $3n+2$ and use algebraic techniques to show that the expression can be written as $2b$, where $b$ is an integer.\n- **Step 4:** Since $3n+2 = 2b$ is a multiple of $2$, we conclude that it's even (i.e., that $Q$ is true).\n\nNote the following:\n\n- For now, we're simply constructing a \"bird's-eye view\" of how direct proof works. We haven't yet proved anything! We'll get to the details of the proof shortly.\n- The example concerns a statement of the form\n$\\textrm{even}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{even}$.\nHowever, we can use the same proof template to prove parity statements of the following forms:\n$\\textrm{even}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{odd}, \\qquad \\textrm{odd}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{even}, \\qquad \\textrm{odd}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{odd}$\nThe only difference is that we'll also need to use the definition of an odd integer. Let's see an example of this." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186158590 1064 "**Direct proof** is a technique for proving that an implication $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is true. We start with a true statement $P$, and then use definitions, algebraic techniques, and logic to demonstrate that $Q$ must also be true.\n\nThe outline of a typical direct proof is the following:\n\n> **Statement.** *If $P$, then $Q$.*\n> *Proof.*\n> > **Assumption:** *Suppose $P$ is true.*\n> > *Explanation of what $P$ means using axioms, definitions, or other results.*\n> > *Algebraic techniques, logic, and further definitions or axioms are used to demonstrate, through a series of inferences, that $P$ implies $Q$.*\n> > **Conclusion:** *Therefore, $Q$ is true.*\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to prove parity statements using direct proof." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186158590 1064 "**Direct proof** is a technique for proving that an implication $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is true. We start with a true statement $P,$ and then use definitions, algebraic techniques, and logic to demonstrate that $Q$ must also be true. \n\nThe outline of a typical direct proof is the following:\n\n**Statement.** *If $P,$ then $Q.$* \n\n*Proof.* \n\n**Assumption:** *Suppose $P$ is true.*\n\n*Explanation of what $P$ means using axioms, definitions, or other results.*\n\n*Algebraic techniques, logic, and further definitions or axioms are used to demonstrate, through a series of inferences, that $P$ implies $Q.$* \n\n**Conclusion:** *Therefore, $Q$ is true.*\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to prove parity statements using direct proof." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186158591 1064 "Different mathematical results have different names. Let's go through those that are commonly used.\n\n- A **theorem** is a major result for which a mathematical proof exists (e.g., the Pythagorean Theorem).\n\n- A **proposition** is a result that is less important than a theorem for which a mathematical proof exists.\n\n- A **lemma** is a small, intermediate result or proposition often used as a stepping stone in proving a larger theorem.\n\n- A **corollary** is a smaller result that follows from a theorem. This is often an important special case of a theorem.\n\n- A **conjecture** is a result that some people believe to be true, but no valid proof currently exists.\n\nOne example of a famous conjecture is the **twin prime conjecture.** A twin prime pair is an ordered pair $(p,p+2)$ such that $p$ and $p+2$ are both prime. The first few twin primes are:\n\n$$\n(3, 5), \\quad (5,7), \\quad (11,13), \\quad (17,19), \\quad ...\n$$\n\nIt's conjectured that there are infinitely many twin primes. Many mathematicians believe this to be true, but no proof of the twin prime conjecture currently exists." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186158591 1064 "Different mathematical results have different names. Let's go through those that are commonly used.\n\n- A **theorem** is a major result for which a mathematical proof exists (e.g., the Pythagorean Theorem).\n- A **proposition** is a result that is less important than a theorem for which a mathematical proof exists.\n- A **lemma** is a small, intermediate result or proposition often used as a stepping stone in proving a larger theorem.\n- A **corollary** is a smaller result that follows from a theorem. This is often an important special case of a theorem.\n- A **conjecture** is a result that some people believe to be true, but no valid proof currently exists.\n\nOne example of a famous conjecture is the **twin prime conjecture.** A twin prime pair is an ordered pair $(p,p+2)$ such that $p$ and $p+2$ are both prime. The first few twin primes are:\n$(3, 5), \\quad (5,7), \\quad (11,13), \\quad (17,19), \\quad..$.\n\nIt's conjectured that there are infinitely many twin primes. Many mathematicians believe this to be true, but no proof of the twin prime conjecture currently exists." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186158592 1064 "Recall that we wish to prove the following result:\n\n*If $n$ is even, then $3n+2$ is even.*\n\nLet's go through the proof step-by-step. We start by stating that an arbitrary integer, call it $n,$ is even:\n\n**Step 1:** *Let $n$ be an even integer.*\n\nNow, we use the definition of evenness:\n\n**Step 2:** *Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$*\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $3n+2$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\n**Step 3:** *Substituting $n=2a$ into the expression, we get*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+2 & =3(2a)+2 \\\\  & =6a+2 \\\\  & =2{{(3a+1)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=3a+1$ (highlighted above), we have that $3n+2 = 2b,$ which shows that $3n+2$ is indeed a multiple of $2.$ So, we proceed as follows:\n\n*Let $b = 3a+1.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. As a result, we have*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+2 & =2\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{(3a+1)}} \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*which is a multiple of $2.$*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n**Step 4:** *Therefore, $3n+2$ is even.*" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186158592 1064 "Recall that we wish to prove the following result:\n\n> *If $n$ is even, then $3n+2$ is even.*\n\nLet's go through the proof step-by-step. We start by stating that an arbitrary integer, call it $n$, is even:\n\n> **Step 1:** *Let $n$ be an even integer.*\n\nNow, we use the definition of evenness:\n\n> **Step 2:** *Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.*\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $3n+2$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> **Step 3:** *Substituting $n=2a$ into the expression, we get*\n> $3n + 2|= 3(2a) + 2; = 6a + 2; = 2(3a + 1)_(⏟)_(b)$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nb=3a+1\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n3n+2 = 2b\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $3n+2$ is indeed a multiple of $2$. So, we proceed as follows:\n\n> *Let $b = 3a+1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. As a result, we have*\n> $3n + 2|= 2(3a + 1)^(⏞)^(b); = 2b$\n> *which is a multiple of $2$.*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> **Step 4:** *Therefore, $3n+2$ is even.*" 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186158593 1064 "When writing a formal mathematical proof, we usually include only the essential details and exclude any additional commentary.\n\nSo, when communicating the full proof that $n$ is even implies $3n+2$ is even, we could write our proof as follows:\n\n*Let $n$ be an even integer.*\n *Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$*\n\n*Substituting $n=2a$ into the expression, we get*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+2 & =3(2a)+2 \\\\  & =2(3a+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Let $b = 3a+1.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. As a result, we have*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+2 & =2(3a+1) \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*which is a multiple of $2.$*\n\n*Therefore, $3n+2$ is even.*\n\nNotice that we skipped a few steps in the algebra in step 3. This is quite normal. Formal proofs usually only include the steps essential to understanding the core argument. Although, this depends on the author and the intended reader. If the intended readers are new to mathematical proofs, keeping more detail is probably a good idea." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186158593 1064 "When writing a formal mathematical proof, we usually include only the essential details and exclude any additional commentary.\n\nSo, when communicating the full proof that $n$ is even implies $3n+2$ is even, we could write our proof as follows:\n\n> *Let $n$ be an even integer.*\n> *Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.*\n> *Substituting $n=2a$ into the expression, we get*\n> $3n + 2|= 3(2a) + 2; = 2(3a + 1)$.\n> *Let $b = 3a+1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. As a result, we have*\n> $3n + 2|= 2(3a + 1); = 2b$\n> *which is a multiple of $2$.*\n> *Therefore, $3n+2$ is even.*\n\nNotice that we skipped a few steps in the algebra in step 3. This is quite normal. Formal proofs usually only include the steps essential to understanding the core argument. Although, this depends on the author and the intended reader. If the intended readers are new to mathematical proofs, keeping more detail is probably a good idea." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186158594 1064 "Before we look at another example, let's state some results that we'll use in our work without proof:\n\n- The sum of two integers is always an integer.\n\n- The difference between two integers is always an integer.\n\n- The product of two integers is always an integer.\n\n- Any integer is either even or odd (but never both).\n\n- The associative, commutative, and distributive laws hold for all integers.\n\nThese results aren't quite axioms because they can be proved from other, more fundamental axioms. Nonetheless, for our purposes, we can think of them as axioms: we won't prove them. Instead, we'll just assume that they're true. In math, we can use statements that were proved earlier in new proofs without re-proving them each time." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186158594 1064 "Before we look at another example, let's state some results that we'll use in our work without proof:\n\n- The sum of two integers is always an integer.\n- The difference between two integers is always an integer.\n- The product of two integers is always an integer.\n- Any integer is either even or odd (but never both).\n- The associative, commutative, and distributive laws hold for all integers.\n\nThese results aren't quite axioms because they can be proved from other, more fundamental axioms. Nonetheless, for our purposes, we can think of them as axioms: we won't prove them. Instead, we'll just assume that they're true. In math, we can use statements that were proved earlier in new proofs without re-proving them each time." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186376514 1071 "Prove that ${n}^{2}-2$ is odd provided that $n$ is odd." 13194139534460 17592186045417 false] [17592186376514 1071 "Prove that ${n}^{2}-2$ is odd provided that $n$ is odd.\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}-2$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ 