clusion.\n\nTherefore, $15n-3$ is even." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422697 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422700 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422701 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422702 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422707 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422716 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422728 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1069 "q-184162" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1071 "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\nIf $n$ is an even integer, then ${n}^{3}+2n$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nLet $n$ be an {{field-1}} integer. Then, $n=$ {{field-2}} for some integer $a.$\n\nSubstituting {{field-3}} into the expression ${n}^{3}+2n$, we can show that ${n}^{3}+2n=$ {{field-4}}, where $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{3}+2n$ {{field-6}} a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{3}+2n$ is {{field-7}}." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1072 #uuid "24483f16-f633-5136-92ad-93ac714083a9" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422738 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{3}+2n\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nLet $n$ be an $\\text{even}$ integer. Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression ${n}^{3}+2n$ and show it is *even* by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nSubstituting $n=2a$ into the expression ${n}^{3}+2n$, we can show that ${n}^{3}+2n=2b,$ where $b$ is $\\text{an integer}.$\n\nOnce we've shown this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{3}+2n$ $\\text{is}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{3}+2n$ is $\\text{even}.$" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422687 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422691 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422710 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422722 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422726 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422730 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422732 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1069 "q-184180" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1071 "Prove that ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is odd if $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+2n-3={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is {{field-7}}." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1072 #uuid "594c61de-dccf-595b-8086-329e505979dc" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422739 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{2}+2n-3\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =(2a)^{2}+2(2a)-3 \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}+4a-3 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a-4)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-2)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=2{a}^{2}+2a-2$ (highlighted above), we have that ${n}^{2}+2n-3=2b+1,$ which shows that ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2{a}^{2}+2a-2.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-2)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is odd." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422688 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422698 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422704 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422705 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422709 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422717 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422735 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1069 "q-184291" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1071 "Prove that  $9n-8$ is odd if $n$ is an odd integer.\n\nLet $n$ be an odd integer. Then, $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-8 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-8 & ={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $9n-8$ is {{field-7}}." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1072 #uuid "777be974-f06f-56a1-8838-5bb4b1e9b194" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422740 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,9n-8\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nLet $n$ be an odd integer. Then, $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression $9n-8$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-8 & =9(2a+1)-8 \\\\  & =18a+9-8 \\\\  & =2{{(9a)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=9a$ (highlighted above), we have that $9n-8=2b+1,$ which shows that $9n-8$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=9a.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-8 & =2{{(9a)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $9n-8$ is odd." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422689 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422708 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422712 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422718 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422723 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422724 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422725 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1069 "q-184289" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1071 "Prove that if $n$ is an odd integer, then $7n+4$ is odd.\n\nLet $n$ be an odd integer. Then, $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+4 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+4 & ={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $7n+4$ is {{field-7}}." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1072 #uuid "b2a01950-32bd-52d0-9e4d-1134c0e8e132" 13194139534460 17592186045417 true] [17592186422741 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,7n+4\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nLet $n$ be an odd integer. Then, $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression $7n+4$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+4 & =7(2a+1)+4 \\\\  & =14a+7+4 \\\\  & =(14a+10)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(7a+5)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=7a+5$ (highlighted above), we have that $7n+4=2b+1,$ which shows that $7n+4$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=7a+5.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+4 & =2{{(7a+5)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $7n+4$ is odd." 13194139534460 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-01a3b71e-fd8a-5eee-b6e8-a1b83053efe5" 17592186422491 "answer-01c86c12-0941-546c-89ea-348e4d765333" 17592186422492 "answer-029923eb-1887-521d-b691-06f7a68cb1c5" 17592186422493 "answer-0390761a-103f-5fcc-9441-f9c6828bf2fc" 17592186422494 "answer-03b3a505-2493-5ae8-8d0b-17c64f288148" 17592186422495 "answer-04480be9-674f-55f3-80f7-990a08b706c7" 17592186422496 "answer-0477f398-d8f2-5377-ad14-76cb1f284c76" 17592186422497 "answer-04819b66-f993-561a-a850-ffa39a35f87b" 17592186422498 "answer-049e33fd-b927-5862-b5a9-d1cd0b592c87" 17592186422499 "answer-04deba7b-4cf4-5063-9a30-07336cb14299" 17592186422500 "answer-0642515c-d492-59ce-9826-c170a7bdaff0" 17592186422501 "answer-0a45411a-bff7-5d83-bce4-86e977529d48" 17592186422502 "answer-0c2ac483-f1a5-57ec-b17f-4761a0f67916" 17592186422503 "answer-1121103d-07e9-5063-b94e-cfeb51cc70ae" 17592186422504 "answer-1354e8d6-39a2-554a-adc3-90e6489145cc" 17592186422505 "answer-13ff42a5-d7ce-5a98-b0b1-c2e3029de743" 17592186422506 "answer-15ebd7aa-e160-5337-8622-e5ac4ab0e0d1" 17592186422507 "answer-1c892d9c-d7e6-5fc8-a99c-cac62c97c0d7" 17592186422508 "answer-1ce7407a-d763-527c-b581-7b980b286bc1" 17592186422509 "answer-1d1fc5a1-f962-5d09-8e7b-46295f25411e" 17592186422510 "answer-1e2a17c0-b360-5fff-bb94-279caba081fc" 17592186422511 "answer-1eae1f92-18d2-5d20-948b-1e6bbbaec4e2" 17592186422512 "answer-2225d332-2992-54ab-9016-3c8f0c4735e5" 17592186422513 "answer-22c2bed4-21b4-5096-9bad-34a742960ec2" 17592186422514 "answer-240446d5-d72f-58d0-99ca-26cdace2b08e" 17592186422515 "answer-252efee4-133f-56e7-ae91-17da8f194e06" 17592186422516 "answer-26635ccf-9bbb-509f-929a-957b8e61c6d0" 17592186422517 "answer-274ff981-6d0d-5a6c-b76c-cec3319fb0e4" 17592186422518 "answer-2938a77f-638a-598a-920d-939781b66aa4" 17592186422519 "answer-29bd0509-84af-516b-8507-8c7